ype=handoff` and invalid tiers fail closed; optional live verifier `eng/verify-memory-endpoints-live.ps1`/`.sh` must stay opt-in, redacted, credential-env/argument driven, and capable of registering `tinyrustlm-agent`, saving public-safe short-term/status plus long-term/handoff records, searching them back by `memory_type`, posting a public-safe meeting-room probe, reading `meeting-messages` transcript `items`, checking `transcript_probe_found`, requiring `transcript_exposure_flags_clear=true`, and writing `tinyrustlm.memory_endpoints.live_verification.v0` with `secret_material_included=false`
  - .NET MemoryEndpoints meeting-room wrapper tests must prove `MemoryEndpointsRoutes.FromBaseUri` builds `/api/matm` routes, goal-room listing uses `agent_id`, `scope`, and `scope_id`, create-room payload uses `creatorAgentId`, room posts use only public-safe `safeSummary`, read-room uses bounded `limit`, typed `ReadMeetingTranscriptAsync` normalizes the live `items` shape (`meetingMessageId`, `safeSummary`, `senderAgentId`, `createdAt`), transcript parsing rejects raw credential/payload exposure flags, mark-read uses the configured agent id, `X-MemoryEndpoints-Key` does not enter JSON payloads, old six-route configs fail closed for meeting APIs, created room scope rejects non-goal/task values, secret-looking summaries reject, and binary/package surface records the new public types and methods.
  - report-intake memory gate derives expected intake count from active `agent-file-handoff/Improvement/*.md` reports, then requires `docs/report-intake/2026-07-08/MANIFEST.md` to list the same report names, declared count, durable copies, SHA-256 hashes, and byte-identical active-source copies when present; this prevents stale hardcoded counts from blocking valid memory distribution or hiding unprocessed intake drift
  - combined generated selector-root smoke covers one browser-multi-parent-bundle `all` root with f32/q8_0/q4_0 entries, registry entry switching, receipt-preferred sparse ASP1 selected before apply, assembly evidence loaded, active quantization preserved after apply, three-cycle route-soak per-pass requests, five-cycle extended route-soak pressure, and eight-cycle stress route-soak pressure across q4_0/q8_0/f32 cycles
future_browser_tests:
  - longer performance soak across more than three cycles and more models when quality-model artifacts exist
  - server-port race test that starts a local service between port resolution and Rust local-server launch
current_dotnet_p2p_seed_host_tdd:
  manifest: docs/report-intake/2026-07-13/dotnet-p2p-seed-host/MANIFEST.md
  synthesis: docs/report-intake/2026-07-13/dotnet-p2p-seed-host/tinyrustlm-dotnet-p2p-seed-host-intake-synthesis-2026-07-13.md
  plan: docs/report-intake/2026-07-13/dotnet-p2p-seed-host/tinyrustlm-dotnet-p2p-seed-host-intake-tdd-plan-2026-07-13.md
  durable_memory: wiki/runtime-mirust/reports/tinyrustlm-dotnet-p2p-seed-host-intake-memory-2026-07-13.md
  intake_gate: exactly 12 reports, six paired domains, byte-exact preservation, one ledger disposition per source, all required hot routes, then source cleanup.
  release_gate: ABI bounds and allocator symmetry; three executed RIDs; clean consumer and tool install; SourceLink, portable PDB, .snupkg, content allowlist, SBOM/checksums; transactional local-feed preservation; wrong-architecture evidence.
  fleet_gate: exactly two lane processes total; up to 20 models; complete endpoint set per lane; shared read-only bytes; atomic pre-bind; sibling independence; strict routes and resource bounds.
  trust_gate: Rust validates every request, piece, assembly, receipt, external proof, and announcement; external reachability cannot be replaced by local health; single-use receipt replay fails.
  import_gate: identity agreement, bounded no-secret journal, crash grid, verified-piece resume, atomic promotion, final receipt reinspection, and no partial runtime visibility.
  lifecycle_gate: ownership-safe idempotent service control, least privilege, readiness after pre-bind, rolling update, retained models on uninstall, and explicit purge only.
  claim_gate: runtime-smoke-only fixtures stay separate; Qwen remains conversation-proven; no private implementation ships; local lawful use is not silently disabled by catalog revocation.
  ui_boundary: center column transition and render exactly once are separate locked-visual tests; the seed companion is not a second chat application.
  anti_cheat: no test weakening, no fake peer proof, no hidden duplicate renderer, no skipped ARM64 execution claim, and no destructive package-history cleanup.
  release_memory: wiki/runtime-mirust/reports/tinyrustlm-web-companion-production-memory-2026-07-13.md
  release_memory_sha256: ea8c1633e92e9b119840615ee25e0448f356c69f0d8c3def8172a262576bff3b
  implementation_evidence: TinyRustLM JS 56/56; MiniModel JS 12/12; Playwright 28/28 with zero skips; TinyRustLM .NET 166/166; both Rust workspaces pass every all-target test, formatting, and Clippy with warnings denied; all three release RIDs execute.
  package_audit: artifacts/reports/seed-host-package-audit-0.1.10-web-companion-final.json
  package_audit_status: passed
  release_rids_executed: win-x64 native; linux-x64 native; linux-arm64 under qemu-aarch64-static with hash-pinned ARM64 sysroot
  local_feed_gate: version 0.1.10 package and symbol pairs promoted transactionally only after verification; existing bytes preserved with collision-safe history.
  browser_bridge_gate: exact loopback Host/Origin/CORS; one-time pairing; volatile origin-bound capabilities; rotation, expiry, revocation, and mutation replay rejection; no credential persistence or broad local-control route.
  deployment_gate: TinyRustLM and MiniModel deployment receipts pass; 44/44 public hashes match; project `.slm` probes and all retired model/demo routes return 404; zero peers remain active without outside proof.
  active_conversation_gate: first send hides starter/setup surfaces; exactly one response renderer; bounded timeline scroll; anchored composer; desktop/mobile baseline comparisons; no CSS concealment of a duplicate path.
  remaining_external_gates: production signing, NuGet.org publication, outside-network receipt, public announcement, and target-machine service installation require separate authorization and evidence.
